
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">Free Narcan &amp; training</p>
      <h1>Anyone can learn to reverse an overdose.</h1>
      <p>Free kits, free training, no questions asked — in Flagler County since 2019.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="hero__grid">
        <div>
          <h2>What we do</h2>
          <p>
            Narcan (naloxone) is a nasal spray that reverses an opioid overdose. It is safe, it
            is simple, and it does nothing at all if the person turns out not to be overdosing.
            Using it takes about as long as reading this paragraph.
          </p>
          <p>
            In 2019 the Foundation became the <strong>first local nonprofit to secure a grant
            from the Florida Department of Children and Families</strong> to provide Narcan at
            no cost to people who need it. We have been handing out kits ever since.
          </p>

          <h3>Who we have trained</h3>
          <ul class="list-check">
            <li>The Flagler County Health Department</li>
            <li>The Flagler County Tax Collector's office</li>
            <li>School district staff — the first program of its kind in the state</li>
            <li>Families, community groups, and anyone who asks</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="hero__media">
          <picture>
            <source srcset="assets/img/narcan-outreach.webp" type="image/webp">
            <img src="assets/img/narcan-outreach.jpg"
                 alt="The Foundation's outreach table at a community event, with free Narcan kits and information sheets laid out under a canopy beside a Road to Recovery banner."
                 loading="lazy" width="1200" height="1200">
          </picture>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--tint">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section__head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Upcoming</p>
        <h2>Next training class</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="grid grid--2">
        <div class="callout callout--gold">
          <h3>[NEXT CLASS DATE]</h3>
          <div class="placeholder">
            <p><strong>[CLIENT MUST SUPPLY]</strong> We need:</p>
            <ul>
              <li>Date and start time</li>
              <li>Venue name and street address</li>
              <li>Roughly how long the session runs</li>
              <li>Whether registration is required or people can walk in</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="mb-0">If classes run on a regular schedule (say, first Friday monthly),
            tell us that instead and we will publish the pattern so this never goes stale.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="card">
          <h3>Request a training</h3>
          <p>
            We will come to your workplace, church, civic club, or neighbourhood group and run a
            session. There is no charge, and participants leave with a kit.
          </p>
          <p>
            Sessions run about twenty minutes for the basics — recognising an overdose,
            administering the spray, calling 911, and staying until help arrives.
          </p>
          <a class="btn btn--primary" href="contact.html?subject=narcan">Request a training</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section__head">
        <p class="eyebrow">If you are worried about someone</p>
        <h2>What to do in an overdose</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          This is general public guidance, not medical advice. If you are in doubt, call 911.
        </p>
      </div>

      <ol class="steps">
        <li>
          <h3>Give Narcan</h3>
          <p class="mb-0">Do this first. Spray one dose into one nostril. If there is no response
          after two to three minutes, give a second dose in the other nostril.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Call 911</h3>
          <p class="mb-0">Call as soon as the first dose is in. Florida's Good Samaritan law
          protects people who call for help during an overdose from certain drug-possession
          charges — calling is the right move even if there are substances present.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Keep them breathing</h3>
          <p class="mb-0">Follow the dispatcher's instructions. If the person is breathing on
          their own but unresponsive, lay them on their side so they cannot choke.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Stay until help arrives</h3>
          <p class="mb-0">Narcan wears off before many opioids do, so an overdose can return.
          The person needs to be seen by paramedics regardless of how well they seem to recover.</p>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <div class="callout mt-6 narrow">
        <h3>Where to get a free kit</h3>
        <p class="mb-0">
          Find us at community events around Flagler County, or
          <a href="contact.html?subject=narcan">get in touch</a> and we will arrange one. Kits
          are free, and we do not ask why you want one.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--dark">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="hero__grid">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Fund the kits</p>
